<AtTheTable>
  Dealing As then Kh and Kh then As gives the same starting hand, although they
  are different dealing paths. Use ordered counts when investigating a sequence
  or a shuffle, and remember that a starting-hand count will need one more step.
  That distinction prevents accidental doubling before any strategy is
  discussed.
</AtTheTable>

<QuantCorner>
  Order also matters in sequences of market moves: the same collection of gains
  and losses can produce different paths along the way. A permutation model
  makes the ordered paths explicit. It is useful only when its assumption about
  which paths are equally likely matches the experiment being modeled.
</QuantCorner>
